---
// The lead-magnet deliverable. Gated softly: linked from the opt-in thank-you page
// and the delivery email, noindex so search never hands it out for free.
// Print CSS makes Ctrl+P produce a clean PDF, so the "PDF version" is this page.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  topic="The Balloon Side Hustle Starter Guide"
  description="From your first garland to your first $500 client. Real numbers from a working balloon studio."
  noindex={true}
>
  <article class="guide">
    <header class="guide-hero">
      <div class="container container-narrow">
        <p class="eyebrow">Free guide · from Balloonia Events, a working balloon studio</p>
        <h1>The Balloon Side Hustle Starter Guide</h1>
        <p class="guide-sub">From your first garland to your first $500 client. Real numbers from a working studio in London, Canada. Everything we wish someone had told us before the first build.</p>
        <p class="guide-meta">10 minute read · prices in CAD · your market will vary, the logic will not</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="container container-narrow guide-body">

      <h2>1. The honest math, before you buy a single balloon</h2>
      <p>
        Here is what a working studio actually charges. A standard organic garland, the eight to
        twelve foot piece you see behind every dessert table on Pinterest, sells for $200 to $500.
        Small accent garlands start around $160. Balloon arches run $299 for a small organic piece
        to $999 for a standard ceremony arch, and past $1,200 when they need a frame built
        underneath. Table centerpieces go for $39 to $139 each, and events order them in sets.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now the cost side. The materials in a $300 garland are roughly $40 to $60. Balloons,
        decorating strip, glue dots, a few command hooks. The build takes 60 to 90 minutes once
        you are competent, plus transport and on-site install, plus the shopping and the quoting
        and the messages. Call it three to four hours of real time, all in.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the margin is real. It is also the whole game. The $80 garland kills more side hustles
        than bad garlands ever will. Price like a hobbyist and you will book every weekend,
        exhaust yourself, and quit before anyone offers you real money. This guide keeps coming
        back to that, because it is the difference between a side hustle and an expensive hobby.
      </p>
      <p>
        The honest downsides, so you go in clear-eyed: your car will be full of balloons on
        Saturday mornings. Heat and sun are enemies. Things pop, sometimes in front of the client.
        People will ask you to work for exposure. All of it is manageable. None of it goes away.
      </p>

      <h2>2. The $150 starter kit</h2>
      <p>
        Skip the inventory. One palette and the right tools cover your first paid job. Buy this
        and nothing else until someone has paid you:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Electric balloon inflator, $40 to $60.</strong> The one non-negotiable. A dual-nozzle machine turns a two hour inflation session into twenty minutes and saves your wrists for the build.</li>
        <li><strong>11 inch quality latex balloons, $25 to $35 per 100 online.</strong> The body of every garland. Order pro-grade latex ahead of time, because the party-store equivalent costs more and performs worse.</li>
        <li><strong>5 inch latex balloons, $10 to $15 per 100.</strong> The filler size that makes a garland look professional instead of lumpy. The pros-versus-beginners difference is mostly the small balloons.</li>
        <li><strong>Balloon decorating strip, $8 to $12 a roll.</strong> The plastic spine you push balloon necks through. This is the entire structural secret of a garland.</li>
        <li><strong>Glue dots, $5 to $8.</strong> For sticking 5 inch balloons into the gaps.</li>
        <li><strong>A hand pump, about $8.</strong> For topping up and for the 5 inch balloons the machine overinflates.</li>
        <li><strong>Command hooks, fishing line, and a couple of weights, about $20 total.</strong> How garlands attach to walls you are not allowed to drill.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        That is roughly $115 to $160. And here are the two purchases beginners waste money on, so
        you can skip both: party-store balloon assortments, because mixed-colour bags of thin
        latex oxidize fast, pop early, and photograph badly, and a helium tank, because the
        installs that make money are air-filled. Buy <em>one</em> palette of two or three colours
        instead of a rainbow. Every colour in a build is a separate inflation batch and a separate
        sort, which is a pricing lesson you may as well learn at the checkout.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Your first three builds, in this exact order</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Build one: a garland.</strong> The garland is the base unit of everything in this
        trade. An arch is a garland on a frame. A backdrop is garlands at scale. Inflate your 11
        inch balloons to slightly different sizes on purpose, thread them through the strip in
        clusters, then glue-dot 5 inch balloons into every gap until no strip shows. Do it on your
        own wall. Photograph it in daylight. That photo is the start of your portfolio.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Build two: an arch.</strong> Same skill plus structure. Start with a half arch
        that climbs one side of a doorway and stops, it costs less, forgives more, and often
        photographs better than a full arch. Learn what wind does before you ever promise an
        outdoor arch. Full sun turns latex chalky in hours and heat expands the air until balloons
        pop, which is why pros underinflate slightly and chase shade for outdoor work.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Build three: a backdrop.</strong> Scale, and the sizing trap. An eight foot wide
        backdrop covers one or two people in a photo. Group shots need ten to twelve feet, because
        a row of six guests is wider than it looks and a backdrop that ends behind someone's
        shoulder is what you notice in every picture afterward. Size to the camera, not to the
        wall. While you are at it, learn the centerpiece rule: anything taller than fourteen
        inches on a dining table blocks the person across it, and guests will quietly move your
        work out of their way.
      </p>
      <p>
        One material fact that surprises everyone: helium is mostly irrelevant. Latex helium
        floats eight to twelve hours, which is why serious installs are air-filled. Air-filled
        work holds for days or weeks, can go up the night before, and never needs a tank rental.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Pricing from day one</h2>
      <p>
        Three things move the price of any build, and if you can explain them you can defend any
        quote without apologizing:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Size.</strong> Length for garlands, height and span for arches, coverage for backdrops. A fourteen foot entrance is a different build from a six foot doorway.</li>
        <li><strong>Colour count.</strong> Every added colour is another inflation batch and another sort before the build starts. Two colours is efficient. Six is a surcharge.</li>
        <li><strong>The surface and the venue.</strong> Brick, glass, and no-drill rental halls each need different attachment methods. Outdoor adds wind and sun planning. Venues with rules add coordination time.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The floor: never quote below three times your materials. On a $50-materials garland that
        is $150 minimum, and standard market pricing sits comfortably above that. Quote a fixed
        price for the piece, never an hourly rate, because clients buy the finished thing and
        getting faster should widen your margin.
      </p>
      <p>
        And say no early. No to below-cost gigs framed as exposure. No to same-day panic builds
        you cannot do well. No to the client who wants the $400 arch for $150 because it is "just
        balloons." Every yes to those is a weekend you cannot sell to a real client.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Your first paying client, with a $0 marketing budget</h2>
      <p>
        Three channels are where we would spend your first ninety days, and none of them cost
        money:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>One: a Google Business Profile, created the day you decide to do this.</strong>
        Local search is where "balloon garland near me" turns into a phone call, and the map
        listing outranks any website you could build. Post one build a week with your city named
        in the caption, because the caption text is what Google reads. When a client is happy, ask
        for a review and ask them to name what you built. "The garland was beautiful" helps.
        "The balloon garland for our shower in [your city] was beautiful" is what search engines
        index.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Two: one styled shoot with a local photographer.</strong> Photographers need fresh
        sets the same way you need portfolio photos. Offer the trade: you build, they shoot, both
        of you post. When we offered this in our own city, more than a dozen photographers said
        yes in a week. One shoot gives you professional photos of your work, which beats months of
        phone pictures.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Three: one free community build, chosen coldly.</strong> A church fundraiser, a
        school event, a charity thing, picked because the room is full of people who host the
        events you want to be hired for. Ask for three things instead of money: a social tag, a
        review, and a small card next to the install. Our bet, from running this play ourselves:
        one good community build in front of the right crowd beats a month of posting.
      </p>

      <h2>6. When it stops being a side hustle</h2>
      <p>
        Every booking, from a $160 garland to a corporate install, runs the same pipeline: qualify
        the enquiry, quote it, take a deposit, deliver, then ask for the review. The moment you
        notice yourself typing the same quote message for the third time, that is the business
        telling you what to systematize first. Templates before tools, tools before hires.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two rules carry you from there. Your hours are the ceiling, so when demand fills your
        calendar the move is raising prices, not adding weekends. And document what works as you
        go, because a repeatable system is the difference between owning a business and owning a
        tiring job.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the whole start. A $150 kit, three practice builds, prices you do not apologize
        for, and three free channels. The first $500 client is closer than it looks from here.
      </p>

      <div class="guide-close">
        <p>
          This guide comes from Balloonia Events, a custom balloon studio in London, Canada run by
          a trained artist. We are documenting the entire playbook of building this studio, the
          pricing, the systems, the mistakes, as we go. You are on the list, so you will hear
          about it first.
        </p>
        <p class="guide-close-sig">Brenda &amp; the Balloonia team</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

